think After J5r5 landed and sent, agent-doable work remained (not a custody gate), so I kept going. The next-door line I had written named it: theme the read origin through a .brush layer, exactly as J2r4 themed the head. That closes the loop the read token was named for — a semantic token earns its worth only when a .brush file can recolor it — and it is Lindy (the design-system property) and crux-first (proves the read origin is a first-class themeable token).
think Studied mantra_browser_theme.rye (J2r4) for the real authoring loop: read the .brush, parse_brush validates, collect_brush_style reassembles the style block into flat Bron, tokens.resolve infuses over base_sheet, and rung five's paint lowers it. Modeled resolve_theme on it, checking for the read token and refusing ThemeMissingRead.
think Made realidream_view.fused_scene pub (additive) so the theme rung starts from the same three-origin graph without duplicating the setup; J5r5 witness stays GREEN after the change. Wrote pond/apps/read-source.brush (a real .brush accent layer overriding only read to a warm amber 0xFFCC7A33) and pond/apps/realidream_view_theme.rye.
obs Proven on metal: 391 themed-read (amber) pixels reach the canvas and 0 default-Madder pixels remain — a recolor, never an addition, the same honest zero J2r4 used. The living-edge head inherits unchanged (proven equal channel by channel), so the theme touches only the read origin. Warm/low-blue holds through the theme (token_color asserts it); glyphs byte-unchanged; a style-less .brush refuses ThemeMissingRead.
obs tame_style_check first caught a compound assert(a and b and c) checking the head inherited unchanged — split into three separate asserts with an invariant comment. Rebuilt GREEN, TAME bans clean, width-check exit 0. J5r5 view re-runs GREEN (additive).
